From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:45:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] check the running order by release date and offer what to
 watch instead

Only episode N-1 of the playing episode's own season was checked: never
the end of the previous season, and season 0 was a season like any
other, so a special was checked against the special numbered before it
and never against the story it airs in.

Order the whole show: regular episodes by season and number, a special
where the scraper says it airs (specialsortseason/specialsortepisode)
or, as TMDB never says, after the last episode released on or before
its own release date. A special with neither has no place and is not
checked; the epoch date scrapers write when they have none is no date.

The check looks at every episode before the playing one, not just one
step back, from the show's first regular episode on: specials released
before the show never stop a first episode. A skip from long ago counts
too - mostly it was never a choice, the special nobody said was there -
and marking it watched settles it. An episode watched before, or one with a resume point,
is not checked. Copies of one episode count as watched if one is.

When something was skipped, a dialog lists it - a special marked as
one, with its year - with three buttons: play the first of them first,
play this one now, or more. Playing the first of them plays that one
episode instead, from its resume point; nothing is queued, the next try
at the one that was started asks again about what is still missed. More offers to tick the
ones already seen elsewhere, which marks them watched and asks again
about the rest; never to check the show again; or to stop. Back stops.
Episodes missing from the library are still reported, now every one
before the playing one in its season.

A setting turns the specials off. The ignored-shows action runs $ID, so
the add-on can be installed under another id.
